tes had issued  against one another saying here's why  we're going to war and they would say  things like you interfered with our  trade relations so we're going to war or  they would say things like we you are  failing to repay debt and so we're going  to war over that or you're getting too  powerful and we're afraid you're  eventually going to Nevada so we're  going to work there's there was even a  manifesto for the u.s. invading Mexico  so in the spanish-american war  mexican-american war the United States  went to war against Mexico because  Mexico failed to repay nearly two  million dollars in debts many people  think of that war as having been caused  by skirmishes on the border and that was  a contributing factor but really the  legal reason for going to war was  because Mexico had agreed to repay a  series of debts and then defaulted on  the debt repayments and the u.s. went to  war to claim territory much of what's  now California and New Mexico much of  southwestern United States is now part  of the United States because we were we  went to war to reclaim that territory as  repayment for the debt okay so tell us a  little bit more of the act that changed  to bring about this new world order yeah  so as I said war was perfectly legal and  legitimate for hundreds of years  World War one was an old world order war  States went to war against one another  over a variety of grievances some of  them bigger than others some of them  were petty and at the end of the war  which was brutal and bloody and millions  of people had died there was a  widespread effort to try and find a way  to end the war and there was a man whose  name was salmon Levinson who was a  bankruptcy lawyer in Chicago who we'd  never heard of before but we realized he  was a guy who came up with this idea of  outlawing more he thought war was  terribly destructive it was a waste and  that the if the countries of the world  could get together and agree to outlaw  war then  then things might change and he created  an organization the foundation for  outlawry of war he worked together with  politicians and peace activists to get  this idea kind of broadly circulated and  eventually that led to a treaty in 1928  where nearly every country in the world  gathered together in Paris to outlaw war  and they reached renounce war as a tool  of state policy within a year nearly  every nation in the world had renounced  the right to go to war and had given up  this tool that they had used for  hundreds of years to right wrongs now  the problem was that they hadn't really  thought about what comes next  so they hadn't really thought through  how do we then take this promise and  make it a reality how do we take this  idea of outlawing war and find other  ways to achieve the same purposes that  were used to serve you know if a state  fails to repay a debt how are we going  to collect on that debt if someone  interferes to their trade lations how  are we going to address that if a state  breaks the rules and conquerors  territory what are we going to do about  it they hadn't really thought that  through but they had taken the really  important first step of outlying war  okay and what was the next step so the  next step was trying to figure out okay  if war is no longer legal what are we  going to do instead and so in 1931 Japan  invaded Manchuria and the world had to  figure out how are we going to enforce  this pact  we've just renounce the right to go to  war can we go to war to enforce the  prohibition of war how are we going to  enforce this rule and Henry Stimson who  had been a classmate of salmon  Levinson's here at Yale he had been in  corresponding with salmon Levinson and  Levinson had said well one way to  enforce the rule is just simply to  refuse to recognize that the territory  that a state has seized is theirs so for  hundreds of years whenever a state took  territory as the United States took much  of the southwestern United what is now  in southwestern United States everyone  assumed okay it's now yours and there  wasn't any kind of disagreement about  the legal right so the territory even if  the war was waged for illegitimate  reasons and the big step that Simpson  made and what  became known as the Stimson doctrine was  to say you might take the territory but  it's not yours we are not going to  recognize this as being Japanese  territory we're going to refuse to  recognize and a treaties or agreements  that are brought about as a result of  this invasion and that was really the  first time in human history something  like that had happened and that they  refused to recognize the conquest that  refused to recognize agreements brought  about through gunboat diplomacy and that  had a real impact and changing the value  of taking territory and because if you  took it you couldn't now sell the goods  you couldn't issue passports marriages  aren't recognized all the things that  you get out of having the territories  being part of yours you no longer got  and that was a first step they then had  to figure out all the rest of the pieces  how are they going to manage without  gunboat diplomacy which had been used  for hundreds of years how are they going  to enforce the rule against aggression  is there going to be a crime of  aggression ultimately I decided yes and  that's resulted in the Nuremberg trials  after World War Two and so each of these  pieces they had to be in to put into  place and part of the story we tell the  internationalist that the book is named  for our the people are trying to figure  that out they're struggling with how do  we take this idea that war is not going  to be the way we're going to resolve our  disputes anymore and how are we going to  create a new system with a set of rules  that are going to be effective and what  are those going to look like okay and  let's talk about the internationalist I  believe there's four that you highlight  in your book yeah so I mean there's a  whole cast of characters in the book  there are four key characters one of  them is Sam and Levinson who I mentioned  and he has a colleague James T Shotwell  who is a something of a friend of me  we'd say today they got along but they  didn't always agree about what the  outlaw tree movement should look like  and how it should achieve its goals  Shotwell took the idea of outlawing more  and he's part of the reason that are  stood by on the French Foreign Minister  got interested in the idea and proposed  in fact the idea back to the United  States and so he was one who helped make  the agreement come to fruition  even though Levinson didn't  appreciate his meddling nor did Frank  Kellogg the Secretary's day who was  actually incredibly upset that Briand  had made the proposal and although  Kellogg later won the Nobel Peace Prize  for it he initially was completely  opposed to it and so there are a whole  cast of really interesting characters  some of them are appealing some of them  really unappealing 11 sends one of my  favorite another is Hersh lauter POC 2  was one of the key figures thinking  through all the ways in which the rules  were going to become effective he wrote  the opening statements at Nuremberg he  was a person who really helped figure  out how to put all the different pieces  together including the crime of  aggression and holding the Nazis liable  for waging an illegal war nearly his  entire family had been killed in the  Holocaust and so he was there for the  initial opening argument but he just  couldn't sit through the trial was so  incredibly painful for him and so I mean  all these people are incredibly  interesting and what's fascinating about  it for me is that they are real people  struggling with ideas they don't really  know exactly what to do and they're  trying to figure it out and they're  arguing with one another and they're you  know they've got kind of envy and  they've got their own personal interest  in it and it's so interesting to see how  you know they're they become real  they're real people and I mean they're  the ones who are responsible for giving  us a legal order that we have but  they're just like us and that they have  the same kinds of desires and jealousies  but they're really deeply committed to  an idea which is creating a world where  war is no longer going to be the main  way in which states resolve their  disputes with one another and they're  devoted to trying to figure out how to  make that work ok and what about the UN  what role does the UN play in your story  so many people think all this starts  with the UN so they think ok yeah I see  that the modern League order is  different from the way the old one was  but really that's because the United  Nations and the United Nations is really  important but it plays it what's key to  understand is that the UN is not just  kind of inventing things at a whole  cloth it's not  coming up with the idea for the first  time the central idea is in the United  Nations Charter first appear in the 1928  Calabrian pact and all this effort of  working out what the rules are going to  look like is what makes the United  Nations Charter possible and in fact we  found that James T Shotwell who I  mentioned was the one who kind of worked  on outlawry afore and who was kind of in  competition slash you know agreed with  Sam and Levinson he in fact was a member  of a group formed within the State  Department by another one of our  internationalists Sumner Welles who was  an undersecretary of state in the State  Department and he convened a group  including James T shot well to think  through ok now that Wars broken out  again how are we going to keep the peace  after this war and they come up with a  first draft of the United Nations and in  fact we found a document in the  Roosevelt library with the initials JT s  James Thomas shot well which we believe  is a very first draft of what would  become the United Nations Charter and in  that drafty actually cut and paste the  pacts text directly at the very  beginning then eventually that turns  into the article article 2 for the  prohibition on use of force in the  modern Charter but you can really see  how the ideas of the Charter are coming  not just indirectly but directly out of  the pact and they're building all these  institutions around it to try and make  it effective ok and and looking at the  pact today does it still resonate with  what is going on in the world today  I mean do you think it will survive well  this is a real challenge at this moment  we see these this world order once again  up for grabs and I think what the book  helps us see is the way that things are  is relatively new the idea that war is  an illegal legitimate means of resolving  disputes is relatively new and there's  no reason that it can't change again and  part of this is meant to be a warning  and to say look the world used to look  very different it was much worse  for all the flavors and problems that  the modern legal order and we're very  honest about all of those it's not  perfect but it's so much better than  what came before and be very careful  what you put at risk the efforts to  they're the threats to withdraw from the  World Trade Organization for instance  the pulling back of support for the  United Nations the kind of threats to  use military force in ways that wouldn't  be consistent with the United Nations  Charter these are very dangerous things  to be doing because they put this  achievement of the international that's  very much at risk and what we're trying  to tell people in the book is see what  the internationalists achieve see how  important that transformation wants see  how difficult it was and be careful  before you put that all risk okay very  interesting thank you so much for being  here with us today and sharing your work  thank you thanks so much for having me  for more information about Professor  Hathaway and her research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you  [Music]

